    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1991-p27.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Because \\[ \\frac1{x-\\sqrt{x^2-1}}=x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}, \\] the given equation implies \\(x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}=10.\\) Its reciprocal is \\(x-\\sqrt{x^2-1}=\\frac{1}{10},\\) so adding yields \\(2x=10.1\\) and \\(x=5.05.\\) Also \\[ \\frac1{x^2+\\sqrt{x^4-1}}=x^2-\\sqrt{x^4-1}. \\] Therefore the requested expression is \\(2x^2=2(5.05)^2=51.005.\\)\n\nThus the correct answer is C.",
      "hints": [
        "Rationalize \\(\\frac1{x-\\sqrt{x^2-1}}\\)",
        "The last two terms of the requested expression are conjugates"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
